00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:34.386
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:34.686 --> 00:02:38.113
How do you think technology will have changed by 2035?

46
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:42.693
I think machine learning will have replaced many office tasks.

47
00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:45.402
Will cash have disappeared?

48
00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:50.600
Maybe. Cryptocurrency and mobile payments will have become normal.

49
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:52.896
And schools?

50
00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:58.389
By then, most students will have studied history in virtual reality!

51
00:02:59.683 --> 00:03:02.147
Grammar focus: Future perfect.

52
00:03:02.447 --> 00:03:06.525
Use the future perfect for an action that will be completed before a time in the future.

53
00:03:06.825 --> 00:03:09.591
Will have plus past participle, often with by.

54
00:03:09.891 --> 00:03:14.154
For example: By 2030, AI will have changed most jobs.

55
00:03:14.454 --> 00:03:20.192
We often use by + time (by 2030, by next week, by the time ...) with the future perfect.

56
00:03:21.183 --> 00:03:23.836
Now read and listen. Living in a Data World.

57
00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:27.807
Every day, the world creates an enormous amount of digital information.

58
00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:32.475
Companies use big data and machine learning to understand our habits.

59
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:38.732
By 2030, experts predict, there will have been billions of new smart devices.

60
00:03:40.017 --> 00:03:44.094
This creates opportunities, but also risks such as cybercrime.

61
00:03:45.383 --> 00:03:49.856
Understanding technology will have become a basic life skill for everyone.

